efutation of a certified value     or of an open class's recorded width; never the Python pathwidth     solver; never `solutions_dir=None`; nothing written under `solutions/`),     the bounds section (what `_lower_bound` computes; the expansion bound a     net loss *inside the search*, which is about the solver's clock and not     about the bound's strength; the bound-defeating family of trees of     cliques; `optimum > tw_min_fill + 1` on 131 of the 338), the minor     certificates (`reports/minor_bound_verification.md`; `2-3_0` has a     rule-free one, `2-2_0` has none at any size tried), and \"Lessons that     cost sessions\".   - The code the loop reuses: `satisfiability/expansion_bound.py` (Harper by     exact branch and bound under a cap; its docstring on why a heuristic     inner minimum is a *wrong answer*, not a slow bound),     `satisfiability/relaxation.py` (`minor_sequence`, `minor_groups`,     `MinorConfig`), `satisfiability/mosp_solver.py::_lower_bound`,     `pathwidth_solver/cli/README.md` and `pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/minor.py`'s     docstring (the contraction rules and `replay`; read, never run the     Python solver), `learning/bound_harness.py` (every floor goes through     it; `family_separators`, `linked_third`, `--extra`), `learning/proofs.py`     (CaDiCaL with proof, `drat-trim`), `reports/scratch/minor_certificates/`     (`mc_graph.py`'s independent replay, `vsmemo.c`, `checkF.c`;     `vsanneal.c` anneals layouts, not contractions), `learning/first_moment.py::vertex_separation_brute`,     `learning/extremal.py::local_search`.   - The Lean tools: `lean/MOSPFormalization/EmptyPair.lean`     (`exists_closedNbhd_card_le`, `emptyPairIndex`,     `card_sub_two_mul_emptyPairIndex_sub_two_le_pathwidth`), `Minor.lean`     (`IsContractionSeq`, `minorOf`, `pathwidth_minorOf_le`,     `pathwidth_gt_of_minorOf`, `lt_mospValue_of_minorOf`), `Sandwich.lean`     (`branch_lemma`, `branch_lemma_separator`, `minDegreeIn_le_degeneracy`,     `degeneracy_le_pathwidth`, `treewidth_le_pathwidth`), `MOSPGraph.lean`     (`card_le_pathwidth_add_one_of_isClique`, `mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one`),     `PendantCliques.lean`; `Search/BetterMove.lean` for the `decide +kernel`     pattern; `paper1/axiom_check.lean`; `learning/sandwich.py`. - **The object.** For a graph `G` on `n` vertices (the MOSP graph of an   instance: customers as vertices, a clique per pattern; or a dataset   class's edge list), `f(t) = min_{|C| = t} |N[C]|`, `s* = max_t min(t − 1, n − f(t))`;   family **E**'s floor is `max_t (f(t) − t) + 1` over proved levels, its   ceiling `max_t (f̂(t) − t) + 1` over incumbents; family **M**'s floor is the   proved pathwidth (+1) of a contraction minor; family **B**'s the   third-largest certified branch width + 1 at a separator. Every value in   optimum units (`pathwidth + 1`); \"gap\" is `optimum − value`; a value that   is not a valid bound is called a ceiling or an estimate in every table. - **Nothing is solved twice.** Every optimum is on record (`solutions/`,   `learning/data/instances.csv`, the dataset). The C program runs on minors   and on branches only, each under a cap, with `-m min_vertices=1000000`   so the certificate is ours; never on a whole certified graph; on the 24   open classes never on a minor above 90% of the vertices. New exact   solves are allowed only where an item says so: subset enumeration at   `n ≤ 22`, `vertex_separation_brute` at `n ≤ 16` for validation, the   C program on minors and branches, SAT decisions on `(t, b)`. - **Every certificate is checked by code that shares nothing with the   search**: `paper1/obstruction_check.py`, with its own readers, its own   contraction replay, its own CNF generator, its own BFS. A claim in the   report cites the checker's verdict, the certificate's path and the Lean   theorem the certificate rests on. A certificate the checker rejects is a   finding, not a nuisance. - **State before you prove, and check before you state.** Every Lean   statement is first che